    {
      "skill_id": "en-a2-018",
      "title": "Quantifiers with count and non-count nouns",
      "explanation": "Count nouns (apples, books) can be counted; non-count nouns (water, money) cannot. Use many and a few with count nouns: many books, a few apples. Use much and a little with non-count nouns: much water, a little milk. Use a lot of and some with both. In questions and negatives use many and much: How many eggs? How much sugar? There aren't many chairs.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "There are a few apples on the table."
        },
        {
          "en": "How much sugar do you want?"
        },
        {
          "en": "We have a lot of time."
        }
      ],
