      "skill_id": "en-a2-020",
      "title": "Adverbs of frequency and manner in simple clauses",
      "explanation": "Adverbs of frequency (always, usually, often, sometimes, never) go before the main verb but after be: She often reads. He is always late. Adverbs of manner (quickly, carefully, well) usually go after the verb or after its object: He drives carefully. She speaks English well. Do not put a manner adverb between the verb and its object: not She speaks well English.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "She often reads in bed."
        },
        {
          "en": "He is always late."
        },
        {
          "en": "She speaks English well."
        }
      ],
